1.3 Core Principle 1 — Scarcity, Choice, and Opportunity Cost
Every choice implies an opportunity cost—the value of the next best alternative forgone: \[\begin{equation}
\text{Economic Cost} = \text{Explicit Out-of-Pocket Outlays} + \text{Implicit Opportunity Costs}
\end{equation}\]






NoteThe Opportunity Cost of Higher Education



An undergraduate student pays $12,000 per year in tuition and fees. If that student foregoes a $40,000 full-time salary, the true economic cost of attendance is $52,000 per year ($12,000 + $40,000), not merely the direct accounting expenditure.

2.1 The Production Possibilities Frontier (PPF) Model
The PPF illustrates the boundary of maximum output combinations achievable under fixed technology and full factor utilization. \[\begin{equation}
MRT = -\frac{\Delta Y}{\Delta X} = \left|\frac{dY}{dX}\right| = \frac{\text{Quantity of Good } Y \text{ Forgone}}{\text{Quantity of Good } X \text{ Gained}}
\end{equation}\]

3.2 Equilibrium and Vector Shifts
Equating \(Q_d = Q_s\): \[\begin{equation}
P^* = \frac{a - c}{b + d}, \qquad Q^* = \frac{ad + bc}{b + d}
\end{equation}\]






NoteClear It Up: Shifts vs. Movements Along Curves



Changes in own-price cause movements along the static schedule (change in quantity demanded/supplied). Non-price determinants shift the schedule itself (change in demand/supply).

4.1 Surplus Metrics
Consumer Surplus (\(CS\)) is the area under inverse demand above market price: \[\begin{equation}
CS = \int_0^{Q^*} [P(q) - P^*] \, dq = \frac{1}{2} Q^* (P_{\text{choke}} - P^*)
\end{equation}\] Producer Surplus (\(PS\)) is the area above inverse supply below market price: \[\begin{equation}
PS = \int_0^{Q^*} [P^* - MC(q)] \, dq = \frac{1}{2} Q^* (P^* - P_{\text{min}})
\end{equation}\] Total Surplus (\(TS = CS + PS\)) is maximized at competitive market equilibrium where \(MSB = MSC\).

5.1 Midpoint Arc Elasticity Formula
\[\begin{equation}
\varepsilon_d = \frac{(Q_2 - Q_1) / [(Q_1 + Q_2)/2]}{(P_2 - P_1) / [(P_1 + P_2)/2]}
\end{equation}\] Demand is classified as elastic if \(|\varepsilon_d| &gt; 1\), unit elastic if \(|\varepsilon_d| = 1\), and inelastic if \(|\varepsilon_d| &lt; 1\).

11.2 Constrained Optimization
Utility is maximized at the tangency point where \(MRS\) equals the price ratio: \[\begin{equation}
\frac{MU_x}{P_x} = \frac{MU_y}{P_y} \iff MRS = \frac{P_x}{P_y}
\end{equation}\] Price changes decompose into a substitution effect (movement along indifference curve) and an income effect (parallel budget shift).

1.2 Economic Modeling and Ceteris Paribus
To analyze human behavior, economists formulate models that isolate causal mechanisms: \[\begin{equation}
\text{Ceteris Paribus} \equiv \text{"All other things held constant"}
\end{equation}\]

2.3 Ricardian Comparative Advantage
Comparative advantage is determined strictly by lower domestic opportunity cost. ::: {.callout-note title=“Terms of Trade Rule”}
Mutually beneficial exchange requires the Terms of Trade (\(TOT\)) to settle strictly between both domestic opportunity cost ratios: \[\begin{equation}
\left|\frac{\Delta Y}{\Delta X}\right|_{\text{Region A}} &lt; TOT &lt; \left|\frac{\Delta Y}{\Delta X}\right|_{\text{Region B}}
\end{equation}\] Specialization and trade shift the Consumption Possibilities Frontier (\(CPF\)) strictly outside the domestic autarky PPF.
:::
